\n  {\n   \"anchors\": [\n    {\n     \"lineEnd\": 9,\n     \"lineStart\": 5,\n     \"path\": \"App.Api/SeedData.cs\",\n     \"quote\": \"public static class SeedData\\n{\\n    public static void Run(AppDb db)\\n    {\\n    }\",\n     \"symbol\": \"SeedData.Run\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"lineEnd\": 2585,\n     \"lineStart\": 2575,\n     \"path\": \"contracts/openapi.yaml\",\n     \"quote\": \"          example:\\n            items:\\n              - id: 3101\\n                name: \\\"Cedar Clinic receipt\\\"\\n                status: \\\"Quarantined\\\"\\n                productName: \\\"Vaccine Pack\\\"\\n                lotNumber: \\\"LOT-2026-041\\\"\\n                expiryDate: \\\"2027-06-30\\\"\\n                quantity: 24\",\n     \"symbol\": \"Collection example\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"lineEnd\": 7,\n     \"lineStart\": 5,\n     \"path\": \"App.Api/AppDb.cs\",\n     \"quote\": \"public class AppDb(DbContextOptions<AppDb> options) : DbContext(options)\\n{\\n}\",\n     \"symbol\": \"AppDb\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"id\": \"C-6\",\n   \"spans\": [\n    \"I-6\"\n   ],\n   \"text\": \"`SeedData.Run` currently has an empty body, so it writes no sample rows. The contract does include example response data—for instance a Cedar Clinic receipt—but those examples are not currently persisted by the seeder; `AppDb` also declares no entities/tables.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"anchors\": [\n    {\n     \"lineEnd\": 24,\n     \"lineStart\": 20,\n     \"path\": \"App.Api/Program.cs\",\n     \"quote\": \"        using var scope = app.Services.CreateScope();\\n        var db = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<AppDb>();\\n        db.Database.EnsureCreated();\\n        // sample rows, only when asked for (Seed__Demo=true): tests and the factory's own checks start on an empty database\\n        if (app.Configuration.GetValue<bool>(\\\"Seed:Demo\\\")) SeedData.Run(db);\",\n     \"symbol\": \"Program\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"lineEnd\": 9,\n     \"lineStart\": 3,\n     \"path\": \"App.Api/SeedData.cs\",\n     \"quote\": \"// Sample rows for a person trying the product. The app calls Run at startup, after the tables exist, only when the setting\\n// Seed:Demo is true (the factory's run files set it; tests never do). Add rows to a table only when that table is empty.\\npublic static class SeedData\\n{\\n    public static void Run(AppDb db)\\n    {\\n    }\",\n     \"symbol\": \"SeedData.Run\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"lineEnd\": 17,\n     \"lineStart\": 12,\n     \"path\": \"App.Tests/HealthTests.cs\",\n     \"quote\": \"    [Fact]\\n    public async Task ApiIsUp()\\n    {\\n        var res = await _factory.CreateClient().GetAsync(\\\"/\\\");\\n        Assert.Equal(HttpStatusCode.OK, res.StatusCode);\\n    }\",\n     \"symbol\": \"HealthTests.ApiIsUp\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"id\": \"C-7\",\n   \"spans\": [\n    \"I-7\"\n   ],\n   \"text\": \"Database creation runs during application startup, and seeding is gated by `Seed:Demo`; the source comments say tests do not set it. The current test only requests `/` and checks for HTTP 200, with no assertion about sample rows. Since `SeedData.Run` is empty, enabling the setting currently still inserts nothing.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"anchors\": [\n    {\n     \"lineEnd\": 11,\n     \"lineStart\": 10,\n     \"path\": \"App.Api/Program.cs\",\n     \"quote\": \"// the web app's address: 3000 unless the setting Cors:Origin names another (the factory sets it when it starts the web app on another port)\\nbuilder.Services.AddCors(o => o.AddDefaultPolicy(p => p.WithOrigins(builder.Configuration[\\\"Cors:Origin\\\"] ?? \\\"http://localhost:3000\\\").AllowAnyHeader().AllowAnyMethod()));\",\n     \"symbol\": \"Program startup\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"lineEnd\": 31,\n     \"lineStart\": 29,\n     \"path\": \"App.Api/Program.cs\",\n     \"quote\": \"// The API's own document (/openapi/v1.json) and a page to read and try it (/swagger). Keep both: the factory's product page links to the page.\\napp.MapOpenApi();\\napp.UseSwaggerUI(o => o.SwaggerEndpoint(\\\"/openapi/v1.json\\\", \\\"API\\\"));\",\n     \"symbol\": \"Program\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"id\": \"C-8\",\n   \"spans\": [\n    \"I-8\"\n   ],\n   \"text\": \"The API configures CORS for `Cors:Origin` (defaulting to `http://localhost:3000`) and its registered UI is Swagger at `/swagger`, backed by `/openapi/v1.json`; no portal screens are part of the current API behavior.\"\n  }\n ],\n \"header\": {\n  \"createdAt\": \"2026-10-13T12:24:54.144Z\",\n  \"inputsHash\": \"\",\n  \"kind\": \"current-behaviour\",\n  \"producedBy\": {\n   \"model\": \"gpt-6-luna\",\n   \"stage\": \"ground\"\n  },\n  \"runId\": \"20261013-meridian-coldchain-platform-in-1d97\",\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1\n },\n \"notFound\": [\n  {\n   \"searched\": [\n    \"Route mappings/controllers/handlers in App.Api/**/*.cs: MapGet, MapPost, MapPut, MapPatch, MapDelete, HttpGet, HttpPost, HttpPut, HttpPatch, HttpDelete; found only the root health route.\",\n    \"Contract paths and operationIds in contracts/openapi.yaml; found the specified operations, but no implementation mappings in the API source.\"\n   ],\n   \"span\": \"I-2\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"searched\": [\n    \"DbSet declarations and entity/model classes in App.Api/**/*.cs; found only an empty AppDb context and no entity sets.\",\n    \"Database read/write logic in App.Api/**/*.cs; found only PostgreSQL setup and EnsureCreated startup logic.\"\n   ],\n   \"span\": \"I-3\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"searched\": [\n    \"Route mappings/controllers/handlers in App.Api/**/*.cs for the contract operations; found only the root health route.\",\n    \"Customer order operation in contracts/openapi.yaml; found changeCustomerOrder, but no corresponding API handler.\"\n   ],\n   \"span\": \"I-4\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"searched\": [\n    \"Seed:Demo and SeedData.Run in App.Api; found the startup gate and an empty Run method.\",\n    \"Sample/example blocks in contracts/openapi.yaml; found response examples but no corresponding sample-row inserts in SeedData.cs.\"\n   ],\n   \"span\": \"I-6\"\n  }\n ]\n}\n</artifact>\n\n<artifact id=\"sketches\" kind=\"sketches\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"spans\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-1\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     {\n      \"text\": \"The existing .NET API application serves the product’s API behavior; clients interact with it through HTTP API operations rather than through a screen built in this project.\",\n      \"kind\": \"happy\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-2\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     {\n      \"text\": \"A client can invoke each operation in the locked OpenAPI contract using its declared method, path, parameters, headers, and request body, and receives the response shape and status codes declared for that operation.\",\n      \"kind\": \"happy\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"The contract’s staff-authorization and receipt-listing/creation operations are available at their declared routes and behave according to their declared inputs, outputs, and access rules.\",\n      \"kind\": \"happy\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"A client can call PATCH /customer/orders/{orderId} (changeCustomerOrder) with the contract-defined request and receive the contract-defined result for changing that order.\",\n      \"kind\": \"happy\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Requests that fail a contract-defined validation, authorization, or resource-existence condition receive the corresponding contract-defined error response; operations do not silently accept invalid or unauthorized changes.\",\n      \"kind\": \"error\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat contracts/openapi.yaml as the authoritative, exhaustive definition of API behavior: implement its operations, paths, methods, request and response shapes, status codes, and declared security; use the request’s user stories only as context, not as permission to add undocumented endpoints or alter the contract.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-3\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Data created or changed through a contracted operation is stored and is available to subsequent contracted operations that list, retrieve, or change that data.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Creating a receipt through the contracted receipt-creation operation records it so that the contracted receipt-listing operation can return it; changing an order through the contracted order operation is reflected in later reads of that order.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"The API preserves the relationships and fields required by the contract when it stores and returns records, so repeated reads do not lose or fabricate contract-defined data.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"If the configured database connection is missing or the database cannot be initialized, the application does not report a successful startup with usable but non-persistent API operations.\",\n      \"kind\": \"error\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Implement persistence for the data required by the contracted operations, rather than treating the broader solution overview as a requirement to implement uncontracted product capabilities.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"spans\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-1\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     {\n      \"text\": \"The existing .NET API serves the product’s backend operations over HTTP; this change does not add portal screens or other UI. API consumers can call the backend independently of the web app.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"API requests that require persisted data read and write the shared backend records rather than returning in-memory or fabricated results. Invalid or unavailable backend configuration does not silently produce successful business-operation responses.\",\n      \"kind\": \"error\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"readingChosen\": \"API-side product work only; no web, customer-portal, or driver-app screens are built in this project.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-2\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     {\n      \"text\": \"For every operation in contracts/openapi.yaml, the API accepts the contract-defined method, path, parameters, request fields and content types, and returns the contract-defined response fields, status codes and content types. Required-field, type, enum and format violations are rejected rather than silently accepted or returned as success.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"The contract-defined authorization operation distinguishes permitted and refused access. Meridian staff access is restricted to Meridian accounts; customer access requires the customer sign-in’s second step. Authenticated users can access only the records permitted by their role, warehouse assignments and customer organisation. Auditors have read-only access; attempts to write are refused. Customer users cannot access another organisation’s orders, deliveries or documents, and customer administrators can manage users only in their own organisation.\",\n      \"kind\": \"permission\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Contract-defined receipt listing and creation return and persist the receipt data represented by the contract. A receipt request that violates contract validation or the applicable business rules—such as an expired product lot or a write by a user without warehouse authority—is refused and does not create a receipt.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Contract-defined customer-order operations, including PATCH /customer/orders/{orderId} (changeCustomerOrder), accept only the contract’s request shape and return its specified result. A customer can change or cancel their own order only while it is before Packed; a foreign organisation’s order, a disallowed status transition, or an invalid change is refused without changing the order.\",\n      \"kind\": \"permission\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"All other operations named in the locked contract are callable at their exact documented routes and enforce their documented request/response and access rules; an undocumented route or unsupported method does not perform a business action.\",\n      \"kind\": \"error\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"readingChosen\": \"The locked OpenAPI document governs operation surface and wire-level details. The supplied current-behaviour summary identifies receipt and authorization operations and the customer-order PATCH operation, but does not expose the full YAML; operation-specific wire fields and statuses therefore remain those defined in that document.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-3\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Persist products and their required temperature profiles, including version, effective date, reason and author. A profile cannot be saved without a product profile or with overlapping tiers or gaps; prior versions remain viewable and cannot be changed. Product search works by name or code, and a deactivated product cannot be ordered.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Persist each stock receipt separately with product, lot, expiry, quantity, supplier and storage location, even when product and lot number match an earlier receipt. New stock is Quarantined; only a Quality Officer can release it, and stock arriving within 30 days of expiry cannot be released. Past-expiry stock cannot be received. Inbound logger data and the resulting tier-by-tier budget usage are retained for the release decision. Stock moves retain who moved it, when, and the source and destination.\",\n      \"kind\": \"permission\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Persist customer organisations, their users and addresses, serving warehouse, minimum shelf-life requirement, orders and order lines. An order must have one or more lines, each for at least one pack, a saved address belonging to that organisation, a delivery date permitted by its warehouse calendar and cut-off, and a choice of partial delivery or delivery complete. A duplicate submission of the same order does not create a second order. Invalid dates, addresses, quantities or cross-organisation requests are refused without creating or changing the order.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"On submission, allocate only Released stock from the serving warehouse, never above available quantity, using earliest expiry first and skipping lots that fail the customer’s minimum remaining shelf life on the requested delivery date; equal-expiry lots with more stability budget used are chosen first. Lines can be split across lots. Complete-only orders reserve no stock unless all lines can be fulfilled; partial orders retain available allocations and back-order the balance. Back orders are filled automatically in submission order when suitable stock is released. Dispatcher overrides require a reason and cannot bypass the Released or shelf-life rules. Concurrent submissions cannot allocate the same stock twice.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Persist order status and per-line allocated and outstanding quantities. Customers can change or cancel their own order before packing; cancellation returns allocations to availability. After packing, customer changes and cancellation are refused; a dispatcher may cancel and receives the applicable allocation-restoration result. When allocated stock becomes On Hold, Rejected, Recalled or Expired, remove that allocation, attempt replacement allocation, and make the outcome visible to the dispatcher.\",\n      \"kind\": \"permission\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Persist warehouse stock quantities and allocation state so available, allocated and quarantined quantities can be viewed by product, lot and warehouse and reconcile to quantity on hand. Stock automatically becomes Expired at the end of its expiry date, is removed from allocations, and the dispatcher is notified.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Persist shipper-box type and qualification, box contents by lot and quantity, seal time and operator, and its single assigned logger. Refuse sealing without exactly one logger, assigning one logger to multiple open boxes, packing products with different storage bands, or packing a product outside the box type’s qualified range. Persist routes with date, vehicle, driver, ordered stops and planned arrival times; refuse conflicting driver or vehicle assignments and confirmation when a box exceeds qualified duration less the configured safety margin, identifying the box and excess. Confirmed-route changes are communicated to the driver.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Accept and persist temperature readings only from the supplier gateway and for known devices. Duplicate readings are stored once; late and out-of-order readings are placed at their measurement time and trigger recalculation. Future readings and readings before box sealing are retained but marked and excluded from calculations. A device with no reading for 15 minutes is shown as silent; an out-for-delivery silent logger raises an alert. Missing intervals remain visible as gaps, not as assumed in-range time.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Persist temperature history for boxes, cold rooms and lots, with readings, applicable bands, excursions and gaps available as chart/table data. An excursion opens on an out-of-band reading and closes after three consecutive in-band readings; tier time is charged to each affected handling unit, combining inbound, cold-room and box history without treating different parts of a lot as having identical histories. Reordered or late readings update excursions, budgets, holds and alerts and retain an audit record of the correction.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"On an excursion, alert the driver when the box is on a route, the dispatcher and the warehouse Quality Officers within two minutes of receipt of the triggering reading. Alert again when a tier’s remaining allowance falls below 25%, when it is exhausted, or when a not-permitted range is reached. Affected stock is placed On Hold pending the quality decision; a late-discovered excursion affecting delivered stock opens a quality-review case and is shown to the customer.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Persist auditable actors and times for consequential changes and decisions, including profile changes, stock movements, allocation overrides and corrected excursion histories. Unauthorized writes, invalid state transitions, impossible allocations, invalid quality or stock actions, and attempts by an Auditor to mutate records are refused without applying the prohibited change.\",\n      \"kind\": \"error\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Treat the described workflows as persisted API data and business outcomes where the API contract exposes them; do not infer unprovided endpoint names or wire schemas. Stability, allocation and access rules apply to the shared records, including when requests arrive concurrently or readings arrive late.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"spans\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-1\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Run the product's server-side API in the existing .NET API project; expose API behavior rather than building portal, customer-portal, or driver-app screens.\",\n      \"kind\": \"happy\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Keep the API's root health response available; it reports successful health with HTTP 200 when the service is healthy.\",\n      \"kind\": \"happy\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Keep the API's OpenAPI document and Swagger UI available as API documentation/testing endpoints; these do not substitute for the product operations.\",\n      \"kind\": \"happy\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"readingChosen\": \"This span means implement the product backend in the existing API project, not build any client application or screen.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-2\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     {\n      \"text\": \"For every operation in contracts/openapi.yaml, accept the contract's specified HTTP method, path, parameters, headers and request body, and return the specified response status, headers and body schema. Do not omit contract operations or add different semantics to them.\",\n      \"kind\": \"happy\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Expose the contract's `/api` operations, including the specified staff-authorization and receipt-listing/receipt-creation operations, rather than treating `GET /` as the business API.\",\n      \"kind\": \"happy\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Implement `PATCH /customer/orders/{orderId}` (`changeCustomerOrder`) with the contract-defined request and response behavior.\",\n      \"kind\": \"happy\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"For invalid requests, missing resources, unauthenticated callers, or callers lacking permission, return the error statuses and response shapes specified by the contract; do not silently accept invalid input or expose another user's or organisation's data.\",\n      \"kind\": \"error\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Do not infer additional API operations, fields, or response behavior from the product narrative when those are not specified by the locked contract.\",\n      \"kind\": \"permission\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"readingChosen\": \"The locked OpenAPI document is authoritative for exact operation-level details. The available context identifies only some operations, so their unprovided schemas and statuses are not guessed here.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"I-3\",\n    \"behaviours\": [\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Persist the records required by contract operations so that creating or changing a record is reflected in subsequent reads, and listing or retrieving records returns the persisted state rather than empty placeholder data.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Keep separate receipts traceable even when the same product and lot number are received more than once; retain the receipt's product, lot, expiry, quantity, supplier and storage location when those fields are submitted through the API.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Persist order and order-line changes, including requested quantities and delivery details supported by the API; scope customer order and delivery reads to the caller's own customer organisation.\",\n      \"kind\": \"permission\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Enforce the API's role and organisation permissions on reads and writes: staff access is limited to assigned warehouses except for Quality Managers and Auditors, customer users are limited to their own organisation, and auditors cannot create, change or delete records.\",\n      \"kind\": \"permission\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Where API operations expose stock receipt decisions, keep received stock quarantined until a permitted Quality Officer release; reject receipt of stock whose expiry date is in the past, and do not permit release when it arrived within 30 days of expiry.\",\n      \"kind\": \"error\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Where API operations expose order allocation, allocate only released, physically available stock, select earliest-expiring suitable stock first, respect the customer's minimum remaining shelf life, and never allocate a unit to multiple orders. Reject dispatcher overrides that violate those constraints or lack a reason.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Persist temperature readings and related history exposed by the API so duplicate readings are stored once, out-of-order readings are placed at their recorded event time, and future or pre-sealing readings are retained but marked and excluded from calculations.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"text\": \"Where API operations expose quality excursions and stock disposition, keep tiered stability-budget usage and holds associated with the affected handling units, and retain changes to corrected histories and decisions in an audit trail.\",\n      \"kind\": \"data\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"readingChosen\": \"Implement persisted data and access rules for behaviors that the API contract exposes; the narrative describes domain constraints but does not authorize inventing undocumented API routes or fields.\"\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n</artifact>\n\n<artifact id=\"differences\" kind=\"differences\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"id\": \"D-1\",\n  \"span\": \"I-1\",\n  \"topic\": \"Root health and API documentation endpoints\",\n  \"readings\": [\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 1,\n    \"behaviour\": 0,\n    \"summary\": \"Only specifies that the API serves HTTP operations; it does not require retaining a root health endpoint or Swagger/OpenAPI UI.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 1,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires a root health response that returns HTTP 200 when healthy.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 2,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires the OpenAPI document and Swagger UI to remain available.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"D-2\",\n  \"span\": \"I-1\",\n  \"topic\": \"Persistence and backend configuration failures\",\n  \"readings\": [\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 1,\n    \"behaviour\": 0,\n    \"summary\": \"Does not state a general persistence or backend-configuration-failure behavior in this span.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 1,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires API requests to use persisted shared records and prohibits successful business responses under invalid or unavailable backend configuration.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"D-3\",\n  \"span\": \"I-2\",\n  \"topic\": \"Authorization and domain-specific access rules\",\n  \"readings\": [\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 1,\n    \"behaviour\": 1,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires authorization and receipt operations to follow their contract-defined access rules, without adding further rules.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 1,\n    \"summary\": \"Specifies detailed staff, customer, warehouse, auditor, and organisation access restrictions.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 3,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires contract-defined authorization/error behavior and protection from other users' or organisations' data, but does not add the detailed rules in sketch 2.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"D-4\",\n  \"span\": \"I-2\",\n  \"topic\": \"Receipt validation and quality rules\",\n  \"readings\": [\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 1,\n    \"behaviour\": 1,\n    \"summary\": \"Limits receipt creation/listing behavior to the contract-defined inputs, outputs, and access rules.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 2,\n    \"summary\": \"Adds refusal of receipts violating business rules, including expired lots or insufficient warehouse authority.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"D-5\",\n  \"span\": \"I-2\",\n  \"topic\": \"Customer order changes and status constraints\",\n  \"readings\": [\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 1,\n    \"behaviour\": 2,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires the PATCH operation to follow the contract-defined request and result, without specifying additional status rules.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 3,\n    \"summary\": \"Limits customer changes or cancellations to their own orders before Packed and refuses foreign orders, disallowed transitions, and invalid changes.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 2,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires contract-defined PATCH behavior without adding the before-Packed rule.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"D-6\",\n  \"span\": \"I-3\",\n  \"topic\": \"Scope of persisted data and product/profile workflows\",\n  \"readings\": [\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 1,\n    \"behaviour\": 0,\n    \"summary\": \"Persists data required by contracted operations rather than broader uncontracted product capabilities.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 0,\n    \"summary\": \"Adds product temperature-profile versioning and validation, product search, and deactivation behavior where not specified by the contract.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 0,\n    \"summary\": \"Persists records required by contract operations and their subsequent reads, without requiring broader narrative capabilities.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"D-7\",\n  \"span\": \"I-3\",\n  \"topic\": \"Receipt records and stock handling\",\n  \"readings\": [\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 1,\n    \"behaviour\": 1,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires receipt creation/listing persistence and order changes to be reflected in later reads, but adds no receipt uniqueness or stock workflow rules.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 1,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires separate receipt records, quarantine and release rules, inbound logger/budget retention, and stock-movement records.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 1,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires distinct traceable receipts with specified receipt fields; behaviour 4 additionally requires quarantine and expiry-based release restrictions when exposed.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"D-8\",\n  \"span\": \"I-3\",\n  \"topic\": \"Order validation, allocation, and lifecycle\",\n  \"readings\": [\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 1,\n    \"behaviour\": 1,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires order changes to appear in later reads, without specifying order validation or allocation workflows.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 2,\n    \"summary\": \"Adds extensive order validation, address/calendar rules, duplicate-submission handling, and cross-organisation checks.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 3,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires detailed allocation, back-ordering, dispatcher override, and concurrency behavior.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 4,\n    \"summary\": \"Specifies cancellation/change timing, allocation restoration, and reallocation after stock disposition changes.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 2,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires persistence of supported order/line changes and organisation-scoped reads, without the detailed workflows in sketch 2.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 5,\n    \"summary\": \"Adds allocation constraints and suitable-stock ordering where allocation is exposed, but not sketch 2's full workflow.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"D-9\",\n  \"span\": \"I-3\",\n  \"topic\": \"Stock visibility, expiry, and warehouse quantities\",\n  \"readings\": [\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 5,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires stock quantities and allocation states to reconcile and automatically expire stock with notification.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 4,\n    \"summary\": \"Specifies receipt quarantine and release limits but not automatic expiry, quantity views, or notification.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"D-10\",\n  \"span\": \"I-3\",\n  \"topic\": \"Temperature and shipment workflows\",\n  \"readings\": [\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 6,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires shipper-box/logger constraints, route persistence, assignment-conflict checks, and duration-qualified confirmation.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 7,\n    \"summary\": \"Specifies gateway/device validation, duplicate and late reading handling, silence alerts, and gap visibility.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 8,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires history charts, tier-budget calculations, excursion processing, and recalculation after reordered readings.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 9,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires timed alerts, stock holds, and quality review for excursions.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 6,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires persisted temperature readings with duplicate handling, event-time ordering, and marking/exclusion of future or pre-sealing readings.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 7,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires exposed excursion/budget/hold data to be associated with handling units and corrected histories and decisions audited.\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"D-11\",\n  \"span\": \"I-3\",\n  \"topic\": \"Audit and mutation restrictions\",\n  \"readings\": [\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 1,\n    \"behaviour\": 2,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires stored records to preserve contract-defined relationships and fields, without requiring a broad audit trail.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 2,\n    \"behaviour\": 10,\n    \"summary\": \"Requires auditable actors/times for consequential decisions and refuses a broad set of unauthorized or invalid mutations.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"sketch\": 3,\n    \"behaviour\": 3,\n    \"summary\": \"Enforces role/organisation permissions and auditor read-only access, but does not require sketch 2's comprehensive audit and mutation rules.\"\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n</artifact>\n\n<untrusted_document id=\"request\" source=\"cli\">\nMeridian ColdChain Platform\n\nIntroduction\n\nMeridian Healthcare Distribution supplies vaccines, insulin, biologics and other temperature-sensitive\nmedicines to around 400 hospitals, pharmacies and clinics. Stock is held in three warehouses (Karachi,\nLahore and Dubai) and delivered by Meridian's own vans in insulated shipper boxes.\n\nEvery one of these products has a storage temperature band, for example 2 °C to 8 °C. A product that\nspends too long outside its band may lose potency without any visible sign. Regulators require Meridian\nto prove, for every unit it hands over, that the product stayed within its permitted conditions from the\nmoment Meridian received it until the customer signed for it.\n\nToday this proof is assembled by hand. Cold rooms are checked on paper twice a day. Each shipper box\ncarries a data logger that is plugged into a computer after the van returns. Stock is tracked in\nspreadsheets per warehouse. Delivery notes are signed on paper.\n\nMeridian plans to replace this with a single platform covering stock, orders, deliveries, live temperature\nmonitoring, quality decisions and recalls.\n\nProblem Statement\n\nThe current process does not tell anyone that a product is at risk until it is too late to act.\n\nAs a result:\n\n● A temperature problem in a van is discovered hours after the product has been handed to the\ncustomer.\n● Quality staff cannot tell how much time outside its band a product has already used up, so they\neither write off good stock or release stock on judgement alone.\n● Stock nearest its expiry date is not reliably sent out first, and customers receive product with too\nlittle shelf life left.\n● Two people working from different copies of a spreadsheet promise the same stock to two\ncustomers.\n● When a manufacturer recalls a lot, finding every customer who received it takes two to three days.\n● Records can be changed after the fact, and an inspector cannot be shown who decided what and\nwhen.\n● Drivers lose mobile signal in basements, hospital loading bays and on long routes, so anything that\ndepends on a live connection fails exactly where it is needed.\n\nSolution Overview\n\nThe proposed solution will consist of:\n\n● A web portal for warehouse staff, dispatchers, quality staff, administrators and auditors.\n● A customer portal for hospitals, pharmacies and clinics.\n● A driver mobile application for iOS and Android that keeps working without a connection.\n● A service that receives temperature readings from data loggers and cold-room sensors.\n● A centralized backend system holding one shared record of stock, orders and temperature history.\n\nMeridian staff will sign in with their Meridian Microsoft account. Customer users are invited by their\nown organisation and see only their own organisation's orders and deliveries.\n\nEach product has a temperature profile: its storage band and a stability budget, which is the total time\nthe product is allowed to spend outside the band over its whole life, split by how far outside it went.\nThe system keeps a running account of the budget each handling unit has used, from receipt to delivery.\n\nStock is received in lots. Customers order by product and quantity. The system decides which lots to\nsend, earliest expiry first. Picked stock is packed into shipper boxes, each with a data logger, and the\nboxes are loaded onto a route.\n\nLoggers report a reading every 5 minutes. When a reading falls outside the band, the system opens an\nexcursion, charges the time against the budget of everything in that box, and alerts the driver, the\ndispatcher and quality staff. Affected stock cannot be handed over until quality staff have decided\nwhat happens to it.\n\nWhen a lot is recalled, the system shows where every unit of it is or went, blocks what Meridian still\nholds, and tracks each customer's response until every unit is accounted for.\n\nThe portals shall be available in English and Arabic. Arabic is shown right to left.\n\nUser Roles\n\nWarehouse Operator\n\nReceives stock, puts it away, picks and packs orders into shipper boxes.\n\nDispatcher\n\nPlans routes, assigns drivers and vehicles, and follows deliveries during the day.\n\nDriver\n\nLoads shipper boxes, drives the route and hands over deliveries using the mobile application.\n\nQuality Officer\n\nReviews excursions and proposes what happens to affected stock.\n\nQuality Manager\n\nApproves quality decisions and starts and closes recalls. Legally responsible for released stock.\n\nCustomer User\n\nA member of staff at a hospital, pharmacy or clinic who places orders and follows deliveries.\n\nCustomer Administrator\n\nA customer user who can also invite and remove users for their own organisation.\n\nSystem Administrator\n\nManages staff accounts, warehouses, vehicles, shipper box types, products and system configuration.\n\nAuditor\n\nAn internal or external inspector with read-only access to all records and the audit trail.\n\nUser Stories\n\nUS-001: Sign In and Access Control\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a user, I want to sign in securely and see only what my role and organisation allow so that\nregulated records are protected.\n\nDescription\n\nMeridian staff shall sign in with their Meridian Microsoft account. Accounts from other organisations\nshall not be accepted for staff roles.\n\nCustomer users shall sign in with an email address and password and a second step using a one-time\ncode. A customer user belongs to exactly one customer organisation.\n\nDrivers use the same Meridian account on the mobile application. Once signed in, the application shall\nremain usable for the working day without a connection.\n\nA staff member may hold more than one role. Each staff member belongs to one or more warehouses\nand sees only stock, orders and routes of those warehouses. Quality Managers and Auditors see all\nwarehouses.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● Staff can sign in only with a Meridian Microsoft account.\n● Customer users can sign in only after completing the second step.\n● A customer user never sees another organisation's orders, deliveries or documents.\n● A staff member sees only the warehouses they are assigned to.\n● A Customer Administrator can invite and remove users of their own organisation only.\n● A removed or disabled user loses access immediately, including on a mobile device that is online.\n● A signed-in driver can open and work today's route without a connection.\n● An Auditor cannot create, change or delete any record.\n\nUS-002: Products and Temperature Profiles\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a System Administrator, I want to define each product's temperature profile so that the system\ncan judge every reading against the right limits.\n\nDescription\n\nA product has a name, a code, a pack size and a temperature profile.\n\nA temperature profile consists of a storage band and a stability budget. The budget is expressed as\ntiers. For example, for a 2 °C to 8 °C product:\n\n● Above 8 °C up to 15 °C: 12 hours in total.\n● Above 15 °C up to 25 °C: 2 hours in total.\n● Above 25 °C: not permitted.\n● Below 2 °C down to 0 °C: 4 hours in total.\n● Below 0 °C: not permitted.\n\nTime spent in a tier counts against that tier's allowance. A reading in a not-permitted range means the\nproduct cannot be released by anyone other than through the full two-person decision in US-010.\n\nProfiles are versioned. Changing a profile requires a reason and takes effect from a stated date. Earlier\nversions remain viewable.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● A product cannot be saved without a temperature profile.\n● Tiers must not overlap and must leave no gap between the band and the not-permitted ranges.\n● Each tier has an allowance in hours and minutes, or is marked not permitted.\n● A profile change creates a new version with a reason, an effective date and the person who made\nit.\n● Earlier versions of a profile can be viewed but not changed.\n● Products can be listed, searched by name or code, and deactivated. A deactivated product cannot\nbe ordered.\n\nUS-003: Receive Stock\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a Warehouse Operator, I want to receive incoming stock by lot so that every unit is traceable\nfrom the moment it arrives.\n\nDescription\n\nStock arrives from manufacturers in lots. For each receipt the operator records the product, lot number,\nexpiry date, quantity, supplier and the cold room and shelf where it is stored.\n\nThe manufacturer's shipment arrives with its own logger. The operator uploads the logger's file, and\nthe system charges any time outside the band during the inbound journey against the lot's budget.\n\nAll received stock starts as Quarantined. A Quality Officer reviews the receipt and releases it, after\nwhich it is available to be allocated to orders. Stock within 30 days of expiry on arrival cannot be\nreleased.\n\nA lot can be in these statuses: Quarantined, Released, On Hold, Rejected, Recalled, Expired. Stock\nbecomes Expired automatically at the end of its expiry date.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● A receipt records product, lot number, expiry date, quantity, supplier and storage location.\n● The same lot number for the same product can be received more than once, and the receipts are\ntracked separately but reported together.\n● Received stock is Quarantined until released by a Quality Officer.\n● The inbound logger file can be uploaded, and the budget used on the inbound journey is shown\nbefore the release decision.\n● Stock with an expiry date in the past cannot be received.\n● Stock within 30 days of expiry on arrival cannot be released.\n● Only Released stock can be allocated to an order.\n● Stock becomes Expired automatically and is removed from any allocation it was part of, and the\ndispatcher is told.\n● Moving stock between shelves or cold rooms is recorded with who, when, from and to.\n\nUS-004: Place an Order\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a Customer User, I want to order products for a delivery date so that my site receives what it\nneeds on time.\n\nDescription\n\nA customer user places an order made of one or more lines. Each line is a product and a quantity in\npacks. The order has a delivery address, chosen from the organisation's saved addresses, and a\nrequested delivery date.\n\nOrders placed before the cut-off time (default 14:00, configurable per warehouse) can be delivered the\nnext working day. Orders placed after the cut-off are delivered no earlier than the working day after\nthat. Working days and public holidays are configured per warehouse.\n\nEach customer organisation is served by one warehouse and has a minimum remaining shelf life (for\nexample 90 days) that any delivered product must have on the delivery date. The customer chooses\nper order whether a short order may be delivered in part or must be delivered complete.\n\nAn order moves through: Submitted, Allocated, Partly Allocated, Packed, Out for Delivery, Delivered,\nPartly Delivered, Cancelled. A customer can cancel or change an order until packing begins.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● An order has at least one line, and each line has a quantity of at least 1 pack.\n● The earliest selectable delivery date follows the cut-off time, working days and holidays of the\nserving warehouse.\n● The customer chooses between \"deliver what is available\" and \"deliver complete only\".\n● The customer sees the order status and each line's allocated and outstanding quantity.\n● An order can be changed or cancelled by the customer until it is Packed. After that, only a\ndispatcher can cancel it.\n● Cancelling an order returns its allocated stock to available stock.\n● Submitting the same order twice by mistake, for example by a double tap, creates one order.\n\nUS-005: Allocate Stock to Orders\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a Dispatcher, I want the system to choose which lots fulfil each order so that the oldest suitable\nstock goes out first and nothing is promised twice.\n\nDescription\n\nWhen an order is submitted, the system allocates stock to each line from the serving warehouse.\n\nAllocation rules:\n\n● Only Released stock is used.\n● The lot with the earliest expiry date is used first.\n● A lot is skipped for a customer if, on the requested delivery date, it would have less than that\ncustomer's minimum remaining shelf life.\n● Between two lots with the same expiry date, the one with more of its stability budget used is sent\nfirst.\n● A line may be fulfilled from more than one lot.\n● Stock is never allocated beyond what is physically available.\n\nIf a line cannot be fully allocated, an order marked \"deliver what is available\" is Partly Allocated and the\nremainder waits as a back order, which is filled automatically, oldest order first, when suitable stock is\nreleased. An order marked \"deliver complete only\" holds no stock until every line can be fully allocated.\n\nA dispatcher may override the system's choice of lot for a line, with a reason. An override may not\nbreak the Released-only rule or the customer's minimum shelf life.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● Allocation follows earliest-expiry-first within the rules above.\n● No unit of stock is ever allocated to two orders, including when many orders are submitted at the\nsame moment.\n● A line can be split across lots, and the split is visible on the order.\n● A \"deliver complete only\" order holds no stock until it can be fully allocated.\n● Back orders are filled automatically in the order they were submitted when stock becomes available.\n● A dispatcher override requires a reason and is refused if it breaks the Released-only rule or the\nminimum shelf life.\n● When allocated stock is put On Hold, Rejected, Recalled or Expired, it is removed from the order,\nthe system tries to allocate replacement stock, and the dispatcher is told the outcome.\n● Available, allocated and quarantined quantities per product, lot and warehouse can be viewed at\nany time and always add up to the quantity on hand.\n\nUS-006: Pack Shipper Boxes and Plan Routes\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a Dispatcher, I want to pack orders into shipper boxes and place them on routes so that every\nbox can keep its contents in range for the whole journey.\n\nDescription\n\nA Warehouse Operator picks the allocated lots and packs them into shipper boxes. Each box has a\ntype, and each type has a temperature range it is built for and a qualified duration (for example 48\nhours) for which it holds that range once sealed. Each packed box is given one data logger by scanning\nthe logger's barcode. A box holds stock for one delivery stop only, but may hold several products and\nlots, provided all of them share the same storage band.\n\nA dispatcher creates a route for a date with a vehicle, a driver and an ordered list of stops. Each stop\nhas a planned arrival time entered by the dispatcher. The system does not work out the best order of\nstops.\n\nA route cannot be confirmed if any box on it would reach its stop later than its qualified duration, less\na safety margin (default 4 hours, configurable), counted from the moment the box was sealed.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● A box cannot be sealed without exactly one logger, and a logger cannot be in two open boxes at\nonce.\n● A box cannot be packed with products whose storage bands differ, or with a product whose band\nthe box type is not built for.\n● Sealing a box records the time, the operator, the contents by lot and quantity, and the logger.\n● A route has one vehicle, one driver, a date and at least one stop.\n● A driver or vehicle cannot be on two routes whose times overlap.\n● A route is refused confirmation if a box would exceed its qualified duration less the safety margin\nat its planned arrival, and the screen says which box and by how much.\n● A dispatcher can move a stop to another route or reorder stops until the driver has left for that\nstop.\n● The driver is told of any change to a route that has already been confirmed.\n\nUS-007: Receive Temperature Readings\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a Quality Officer, I want every reading from every logger and cold-room sensor stored reliably\nso that the temperature history of any unit is complete and trustworthy.\n\nDescription\n\nLoggers in shipper boxes and fixed sensors in cold rooms take a reading every 5 minutes. Readings\nreach the system through the logger supplier's gateway, which sends them in batches. Each reading\ncarries the device identifier, the time the reading was taken and the temperature.\n\nThe mobile network is not reliable. Readings may arrive minutes or hours late, in the wrong order, more\nthan once, or in one large batch when a van comes back into coverage. The system shall produce the\nsame temperature history regardless of the order in which readings arrive.\n\nA device that has sent nothing for 15 minutes is treated as silent. A silent logger in a box that is out for\ndelivery raises an alert. Time with no readings is shown as a gap in the history and is not assumed to\nbe in range.\n\nThe system shall be sized for 5,000 devices reporting at once.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● Readings are accepted only from the supplier's gateway and only for known devices.\n● A reading received twice is stored once.\n● Readings that arrive late or out of order are placed at the time they were taken, and everything\ncalculated from them is brought up to date.\n● A reading with a time in the future, or from before the box was sealed, is kept but marked and not\nused in calculations.\n● A device with no reading for 15 minutes is shown as silent, and an alert is raised if its box is out for\ndelivery.\n● Gaps are visible in the temperature history as gaps.\n● The temperature history of any box, cold room or lot can be viewed as a chart and as a table, with\nthe band and the excursions marked.\n\nUS-008: Detect Excursions and Track the Stability Budget\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a Quality Officer, I want the system to detect excursions and keep each unit's remaining budget\nup to date so that I know immediately which stock is at risk and by how much.\n\nDescription\n\nAn excursion opens when a reading from a box or cold room is outside the band of a product stored\nin it. It closes when three readings in a row are back inside the band. The duration of the excursion is\ncharged, tier by tier, against the stability budget of every handling unit that was in that box or cold\nroom at the time.\n\nThe budget is tracked per handling unit, not per lot: two parts of the same lot that travelled in different\nboxes have different histories. The budget used in the manufacturer's inbound journey, in the cold\nroom and in every box is added together.\n\nWhen an excursion opens, the driver (if the box is on a route), the dispatcher and the Quality Officers\nof the warehouse are alerted within 2 minutes of the reading being received. A further alert is sent\nwhen the remaining allowance in any tier falls below 25%, and again when a tier is used up or a\nnot-permitted range is reached.\n\nAll stock affected by an open or undecided excursion is placed On Hold automatically.\n\nBecause readings can arrive late, an excursion may be discovered, lengthened, shortened or removed\nafter the event. Budgets, holds and alerts shall follow the corrected history.\n\nAcceptance Criteria\n\n● An excursion opens on the first reading outside the band and closes after three consecutive\nreadings inside it.\n● Excursion time is charged to the correct tier, and a single excursion that passes through several\ntiers is charged to each for the time spent in it.\n● The budget used and remaining per tier is shown for every handling unit, with the excursions that\nconsumed it.\n● Parts of one lot with different histories show different budgets.\n● Alerts reach the driver, dispatcher and Quality Officers within 2 minutes of the reading being\nreceived.\n● Stock affected by an excursion is On Hold until a decision is made under US-010.\n● Late readings that change an excursion update the budget, the holds and the excursion record,\nand the change is recorded in the audit trail.\n● A late reading that reveals an excursion on stock already delivered opens a case for quality review\nand is shown to the customer.\n\nUS-009: Deliver with the Driver Application\n\nUser Story\n\nAs a Driver, I want to load, drive and hand over my deliveries from my phone, with or without\nsignal, so that every handover is recorded properly.\n\nDescription\n</untrusted_document>\n\nList the questions."}